\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Paramaters and Steady State Ratios
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
King and Rebelo (2000) provides a detailed explanation on how the parameter
 values were found, as well as the equations to obtain the steady state
 values of the variables.
 In the paper, the momentary utility function was used: 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c_{t},L_{t})=log(c_{t})+\frac{\theta}{1-\eta}(L_{t}^{1-\eta}-1).$
\end_inset

 Once the parameter which governs the labor supply elasticity (
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

) was specified, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 was chosen to match the steady state N, which is about 20% of the available
 time in the U.S.
 postwar period.
 Also, recall the first order condition with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 for this utility function, which was derived in HW 1: 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{(1+\theta)}.$
\end_inset

 Below is a list of the calculations used to find the steady state values
 along with the table of parameters, which can be found in the paper.
\end_layout

