\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Log-Linearization Procedure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next step in solving the model is to approximate its various equations,
 which is most frequently done so as to produce log-linear relations.
 Certain relations of interest were log-linear to start.
 For example, the stochastic component of the model is also log-linearized
 since it can be calculated using the Solow residual.
 Then, assuming that this random variable follows an AR(1) process, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{A}_{t}=\rho\hat{A}_{t-1}+\varepsilon_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore, the consumption efficiency condition is 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}=D_{1}u(c_{t},L_{t})=(c_{t})^{-1}$
\end_inset

 and the leisure efficiency condition is 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{t}=\lambda_{t}\omega_{t}=D_{2}u(c_{t},L_{t})=L_{t}^{-\eta}$
\end_inset

, so that: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $-\hat{c}_{t}=\hat{\lambda}_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.3)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $-\eta\hat{L}_{t}=\hat{\lambda}_{t}+\hat{w}_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.4)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Cobb-Douglas production function also implies that the efficiency condition
 is exactly log-linear,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{t}=\hat{A}_{t}+(1-\alpha)(\hat{k}_{t}-\hat{N}_{t})$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.5)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
i.e., the real wage is raised by productivity and by increases in the capital
 labor ratio.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Other equations of the model are not exactly log-linear and so must be approxima
ted.
 The time constrant is 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}+L_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 so that small changes in labor and leisure satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $dN_{t}+dL_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $N\frac{dN_{t}}{N}+L\frac{L_{t}}{L}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $log(\frac{N_{t}}{N})\approx\frac{dN_{t}}{N}$
\end_inset

, we conclude:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $N\cdot\hat{N}_{t}+L\cdot\hat{L}_{t}=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.6)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the constraint on uses of goods takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}+i_{t}=A_{t}k_{t}^{(1-\alpha)}N_{t}^{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 so that a mixture of the two methods used above yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\dfrac{c}{y}\right)\hat{c}_{t}+\left(\dfrac{i}{y}\right)\hat{i}_{t}=\hat{y}_{t}=\hat{A}_{t}+\alpha\hat{N}_{t}+(1-\alpha)\hat{k}_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \qquad{}
\end_inset

(4.7)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Other equations, which contain variables at different dates, can be similarly
 approximated.
 These derivations are shown below.
\end_layout

