\begin_layout Section*
Problem 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following questions are the continuation from the problem in the first
 homework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Next, we will explore an alternative fix.
 In this question and all the following questions, we will asume that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(C_{t},N_{t})=\log C_{t}-\frac{\eta}{1+\eta}N_{t}^{\frac{\eta+1}{\eta}}$
\end_inset

, but modify the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{t}\equiv\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta}{\theta+1}}\ \ (1^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is now not a simple addition of sectoral labor input but is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

produced
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by the constant elasticity substitution function 
\begin_inset Formula $(1^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $(1^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is a generalization of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=N_{c,t}+N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $(1^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=N_{c,t}+N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 goes to infinity.
 Everything else is the same as before.
 Derive the first order conditions for the planner's problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 cancels out from the optimality condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{-(\nicefrac{\partial u}{\partial N_{c,t}})}{(\nicefrac{\partial u}{\partial C_{t}})}=(\nicefrac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Comovement between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is a necessary condition for the comovement between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

.
 Show that 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 always move in the same direction if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is smaller than some threshold value.
 Be specific to the threshold value in your proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Give some intuition to why small 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 can help sectoral labor comovement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plugging in the modification to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 into the King-Plosser-Rebelo utility funciton, we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C_{t},N_{t})=\log C_{t}-\frac{\eta}{\eta+1}\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta(\eta+1)}{(\theta+1)\eta}}
\]

\end_inset

Then, we obtain the necessary partial derivatives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{c}(t) & = & \frac{1}{C_{t}}\\
u_{N_{C}}(t) & = & -\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}\\
u_{N_{i}}(t) & = & -\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{i,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we rewrite the first order conditions for the planner's problem using
 the partial derivatives above
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\{C_{t}\}:\ \frac{1}{C_{t}}=\lambda_{t}\\
\{N_{c,t}\}:\ \left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}=\frac{(1-\alpha)}{N_{c,t}}\\
\iff\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}=(1-\alpha)\\
\{N_{i,t}\}:\ \left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{i,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}=\mu_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}}{N_{i,t}}\\
\iff\mu_{t}=\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot\frac{N^{\frac{(\theta+1)}{\theta}}}{(1-\alpha)(I_{c,t}+I_{i,t})}\\
\{K_{c,t+1}\}:\ E_{t}\left[\beta\alpha\frac{1}{K_{c,t+1}}+\beta\mu_{t+1}(1-\delta)\right]=\mu_{t}\\
\{K_{i,t+1}\}:\ E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{\mu_{t+1}}{\mu_{t}}\left(\alpha\frac{I_{c,t+1}+I_{i,t+1}}{K_{i,t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right]=1\\
\{\lambda_{t}\}:\ A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha}=C_{t}\\
\{\mu_{t}\}:\ I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}=A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first order condition of the planner's problem with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{N_{c}}(t)+\lambda_{t}(1-\alpha)A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{-\alpha}=0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{c}(t)=\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha}=C_{t}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{N_{c}}(t)+u_{c}(t)(1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}} & = & 0\\
-\frac{u_{N_{c}}(t)}{u_{c}(t)} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}\\
-\frac{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)}{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial C_{t}}\right)} & = & \left(\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}=(1-\alpha)A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{-\alpha}=(1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, from the partial derivatives obtained in part a, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-\frac{-\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}}{\frac{1}{C_{t}}} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}\\
\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 cancels out from the optimality condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{t}\equiv N_{t}-N_{t-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{c,t}\equiv N_{c,t}-N_{c,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is smaller than some threshold value, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 always move in the same direction.
 From part b, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta-\eta}{\theta\eta+\eta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\\
\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta}{(\theta+1)}\frac{1}{\eta}-\frac{1}{(\theta+1)}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\\
N_{t}^{\frac{1}{\eta}}\cdot\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}}\right]^{-\frac{1}{\theta+1}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\\
N_{t}^{\frac{1}{\eta}}\cdot N_{t}^{-\frac{1}{\theta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\\
N_{t}^{\frac{1}{\eta}-\frac{1}{\theta}}\cdot N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\ \ (*)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\equiv\frac{1}{\eta}-\frac{1}{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\eta$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma<0$
\end_inset

.
 Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\geq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\theta+1}{\theta}>0$
\end_inset

.
 So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{t}>0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 decreases.
 Because the right hand side of 
\begin_inset Formula $(*)$
\end_inset

 is a constant, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta}{\theta+1}}$
\end_inset

 must increase, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{c,t}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 always move in the same direction if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\eta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{t}=\left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}\right]^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}
\]

\end_inset

Let us obtain the partial derivatives with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

, respectively
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}} & = & \left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}\right]^{-\frac{1}{1+\theta}}N_{c,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}\\
\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{i,t}} & = & \left[N_{c,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}+N_{i,t}^{\frac{\theta}{1+\theta}}\right]^{-\frac{1}{1+\theta}}N_{i,t}^{\frac{1}{\theta}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Dividing the partial derivatives, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}}{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{i,t}}}=\left(\frac{N_{c,t}}{N_{i,t}}\right)^{\frac{1}{\theta}}
\]

\end_inset

Then, taking the natural log of both sides, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\ln\left(\frac{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}}{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{i,t}}}\right) & = & -\frac{1}{\theta}\ln\left(\frac{N_{i,t}}{N_{c,t}}\right)\\
\implies\frac{d\ln\left(\frac{N_{i,t}}{N_{c,t}}\right)}{d\ln\left(\frac{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}}{\frac{\partial N_{t}}{\partial N_{i,t}}}\right)} & = & -\theta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 represents the elasticity of substitution between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

, i.e., it tells how easy it is to substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and vice versa.
 For higher values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, even if co-movement exists, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\eta$
\end_inset

, there will be still be a decrease in 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is increased.
 However, co-movement would still occur because 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta<\eta$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 would become perfect substitutes and we would have the situation depicted
 in Homework 1, i.e., there would be no co-movement between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

, then the substitutability between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 decreases and the two types of labor become perfect complements, which
 means 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 cannot increase unless 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 increases.
 Therefore, smaller values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 will help sectoral co-movement of labor because 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

production
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of labor requires that the two types of labor move together.
\end_layout

